{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790327540,"nonce":"Gf1KrRP81+rLOwsMZ+VUyZPboRE=","title":"Macro calls ran out of source order; bare calls matched another module's macro; @:const type arguments were dropped","message":"Issue3914: storeExpr(...) calls Context.typeExpr, so it is deferred to lowering where the typer is live. getStoredExpr() needs no typer and expanded in the syntactic pass, before storeExpr had run, so it returned null and the call crashed. Haxe expands macros in typing order.\n\nA bare call `load(...)` inside haxe.macro.Context (a private ordinary method) expanded as any module's macro named `load`: the registry accepts a macro's simple name from anywhere.\n\nIssue6830: `VarChar\u003c123\u003e` lowered its constant argument to Unknown, and TypeTools.toComplexType was not implemented (Context.toComplexType gave null for any spelling with type parameters). Separately, the harness called the macro `testMacro` from the injected static main as if it were a test, which Haxe would reject.\n\nFix: once a macro defers, later calls to macros of the same class defer too, and lowering runs them in source order. A class's ordinary methods shadow bare macro names. A constant type argument is a TypeKind::ConstArgument spelled as its source, and toComplexType parses the type's spelling back into a ComplexType. The harness skips `macro` functions.\n\nConformance 664 -\u003e 666 (Issue3914, Issue6830), 0 LOST.","files":null}]}